# Workflow files stay LF in every checkout.
#
# They are read by Linux runners, and by guards that split them on a bare line
# feed. On 2026-09-16 three separate guards reported false failures in agent
# worktrees, where the files had been checked out with CRLF: one sweep found
# zero run commands in nine workflows and reported success over code it had not
# read, and one job-slicer never found the job boundary, so it read the *next*
# job's secrets and reported a secret in a job that holds none. Three agents
# spent time on it. The files are LF in the index either way; this makes the
# working tree agree.
.github/workflows/*.yml text eol=lf
.github/workflows/*.yaml text eol=lf

# Firestore rules are hashed LF-normalised by the preservation register and
# deployed as text; a CRLF working copy makes the hash disagree with itself.
*.rules text eol=lf

# The reference corpus is held together by hashes: every case source is checked
# against the `Quellenhash:` its case declares, and `manifest.json` carries the
# SHA-256 of the case book it was built from. All three are computed over the
# LF form, so a CRLF working copy would make the corpus disagree with itself in
# exactly the way the workflow files did above.
# The appendix carries its own `MANIFEST.sha256.json` over its own files, so it
# gets the same treatment rather than a CRLF copy that fails its own verifier.
docs/korpus/** text eol=lf
tests/korpus/** text eol=lf
